Alan Frisbie wrote:

> One of our TLZ09 drives completed a backup the other night,
> but then refused to eject the tape.   I've swapped a new
> TLZ10 in its place, but I still have the problem of how to
> get the backup tape out of the TLZ09.

Success!   In case anyone else has this problem, here is the
solution.   First of all, I got this suggestion via e-mail:

   One failure I've seen is where the plastic drive "gear"
   that engages the tape comes off the motor.  When trying
   to eject the tape, this bit of plastic gets jammed causing
   the ejection to fail.  If this is happening, flip the tape
   drive upside down and then eject.  The plastic gear falls
   into the tape cartridge, but doesn't jam anything.  Then the
   tape can come out.  (Of course, it doesn't pull the tape
   back into the cartridge since the drive gear is missing, so
   be careful pulling it out.  You will need to manually wind
   the tape back into the cartridge.)

Unfortunately, this did not work.   Time to disassemble.

After removing the cover on the drive, it was immediately
obvious that the tape was not tangled or jammed.   The reason?
The leader had come off its reel, so the entire tape was inside
the cartridge.   This greatly simplified extracting the cartridge.

On the bottom of the drive, there is a small access hole near
one side, marked "UNLOAD", with a clockwise-pointing arrow
around it.   Just inside this hole is a small screwdriver slot
which is on the end of the unload motor shaft.   Simply turning
this shaft a few hundred times caused the tape to eject with
no problems.   If you have one of those old radio/television
alignment tools, I would recommend it highly because they are
easy to spin between your fingers.

As soon as the tape was out, I applied power to the drive to
allow everything to reset to its normal condition.

I then tested the drive and it appears to be working normally
again.   Now I just have to see if I can re-attach the leader
on the tape that was in the drive.

Thanks,
Alan



