In article <OJydnQ7terIVbq3d4p2dnA@comcast.com>, "Richard B. Gilbert" <rgilbert88@comcast.net> writes:
>There are businesses called "Freight Forwarders" that act as travel 
>agents for freight.  For a fee, they will arrange to have freight picked 
>up just about anywhere and delivered just about anywhere else.  They 
>complete all necessary paperwork, obtain all necessary permits, etc.
>
>If you sell on e-Bay, just add the conditions that foreign bidders must 
>pay in US $ and that they must provide a US shipping address (a freight 
>forwarder who will arrange delivery at the purchaser's expense).
>
>Frankly, I would not expect much interest from overseas bidders; the 
>expense of shipping a used computer to, say, Australia would probably be 
>more than the machine is worth!

This makes things more expensive. It is not that difficult to send things
abroad. The first thing to check is whether the country of origin has any
export restrictions (like the US). If not, you have to complete a customs
declaration. This has to be either in the language of the destination country
or in French, although English is often ok in industrialized countries
nowadays. The next thing is only required if you are friendly to the recipient:
add some invoice (a "pro forma invoice" is sufficient) to your package so that
foreign customs is able to calculate import taxes, if any. If there are taxes
imposed, tax authorities will contact the recipient and request the money, this
is not your business.

The above principle is independent of the carrier you use. Customs declarations
should be availabel at any post office or other carrier's office.

