I wonder if it is vaguely possible that the slightly different path taken
through the conversational boot is temporarily getting you around the
well-documented restriction that all VS3100s will only reliably boot from
system disks under 1.07Gb in size ?  Possible problems include an inability
to boot at all, and (perhaps more seriously) corruptions caused later when
any critical directly-mapped system files, such as the dump-file, are
located beyond that boundary.  You *really* need to know what you are doing
with larger devices to avoid this, sorry.

B/1 is the same as B/00000001.  Putting F in the top byte is a request to
boot from the system in [SYSF.], not [SYS0.]  SYSF is reserved for use in
upgrade procedures.

