In article <opr248s8szrt6s6j@news.central.cox.net>, Chris Proctor <dumont@busoutoshi.net> writes:
>Questions-
>1- Can I use my hobbyist OpenVMS-Vax distro on it? If not, where would I 
>find the correct software without having to rob a bank to pay for it?

If you want to use OpenVMS VAX on it, this is unsupported and may or may
not work. An Infoserver 100 and a MicroVAX 3100 (maybe Model 20) are very
similar but not identical (I was told once, that the Infoserver hardware
has no Floating Point)

So better ask in comp.os.vms, but only after you've read the OpenVMS FAQ

	http://www.openvms.digital.com/wizard/openvms_faq.html

If you want to use it with the Infoserver, you have to find someone with
the Infoserver CD and duplicate it. There are other CDs, too. One to enable
Tape-Functionality, another to enable CD-Writer-Functionality and maybe more.
You can upgrade the infoserver with every #1 (or was it #2 ?) of the OpenVMS
VAX ConDist CD (but perhaps not doing an initial installation from it).

>2- The original DEC drive had the "Dewalt F-disk" done on it prior to my 
>purchace. (ie 13/16 drill bit through the case and platters) and the 
>CD-ROM didn't want to spin/eject/light-up etc. They have been replaced 
>with a 9.1GB Seagate HDD and a RRD43 CD-ROM. Is this a workable setup?

I don't know. The original Infoserver 100 had RRD40 CD-ROM, though RRD42
did and do work, too. I think, an RRD43 could also work.

Regarding the huge disk (9GB), I don't know if it will work.
But if the 3100 restrictions for boot devices (6 Byte SCSI commands in
boot PROM so only max. 1.08GB allowed) apply also for the Infoserver
(which I believe), then you need another smaller disk to boot from
(maybe the 9GB disk will work as a simple data disk).

>3- Is this an item that I would be better off converting into a hibachi or 
>a nice planter?

An Infoserver is a blockserver (versus a diskserver or fileserver) where
a disk or partition of it is served via the network as individual blocks
(this means, the organization of the virtual disk belongs to the client).
It can be used to boot DECservers, DEC X-terminals and even VAX and Alphas
from (the latter ones only for doing an opsys install over the net). It can
also be used for sharing backup devices like DLT drives over the network
but is is unfortunately way too old to be useful as a disk storage environment.

So, its usage is very limited now, but not zero in the DEC world. I used
mine for a VMS installation some weeks ago (it was a Plan D situation ;-)

HIH

